.{
    .name = .plaza,
    .fingerprint = 0xe8703ecc5a707070,
    .version = "0.1.0",
    .minimum_zig_version = "0.16.0",
    .dependencies = .{
        // The Native SDK framework, pinned to the tree matching the `native`
        // CLI (0.10.1 -> tag v0.10.1). `native eject` writes a machine-local
        // `.path` into the npm install here; this git pin is committable and
        // reproducible instead, CI fetches the exact framework by hash.
        //
        // Bump this and the CLI version in .github/workflows/ci.yml IN THE SAME
        // COMMIT. The automation harness refuses to read a snapshot when the
        // CLI and the framework speak different protocol versions, and moving
        // one alone does not fail the build: it silently breaks every live test.
        //
        // A fork rather than vercel-labs/native, for eight patches. Every one is
        // a gap that only shows off macOS, where the toolkit's own software
        // renderer is the whole of what a user sees, and none is fixable from
        // up here:
        //
        //   1. It inks every glyph from one face and stops at U+FFFF, so an
        //      emoji is a solid block. The fork reads cmap format 12 and
        //      COLR/CPAL, so a registered colour face draws real emoji.
        //      Upstream vercel-labs/native#421.
        //   2. It bundles no face for the weight ids it maps spans onto, so
        //      medium and bold ink regular outlines and there is no
        //      typographic hierarchy at all. The fork lets an app fill those
        //      four ids, which is what `registered_fonts` in main.zig does.
        //      Upstream vercel-labs/native#423.
        //   3. A font id resolves to exactly one face, so a codepoint the
        //      bundled faces lack is a solid block however many faces the app
        //      registers. Latin and Cyrillic are covered and nothing else is.
        //      The fork lets any registered face lend a glyph for a codepoint
        //      nothing else can draw, which is what the Noto faces in
        //      `registered_fonts` are for.
        //   4. The GTK present path threw away the dirty rectangle the runtime
        //      had just computed and converted the whole surface every frame.
        //      The fork uses it, which is upstream's own Windows code one
        //      platform over.
        //   5. Every GTK scroll signal ran a full synchronous engine dispatch
        //      inside the signal handler. At the 100 to 200 events a second a
        //      high-resolution wheel or a touchpad delivers, that work outlasts
        //      the gap to the next event, so GDK's input source stayed ready on
        //      every main-loop iteration and the frame emission, which sits in
        //      the idle band on purpose, never ran. Scrolling froze the window
        //      for seconds at a time. The fork coalesces scroll to one event a
        //      frame, which is what macOS has always done.
        //      Upstream vercel-labs/native#440.
        //   6. A detented wheel notch teleported. `applyWheel` adds the delta
        //      to the offset in one step, which is right for a trackpad and
        //      wrong for a wheel: one notch is 40 points in a 690 point
        //      viewport however slowly it arrives, so the feed jumped with
        //      nothing drawn in between and no frame rate could help. The fork
        //      travels it over about 200ms instead.
        //      Upstream vercel-labs/native#441.
        //   7. The software rasteriser did per-pixel work that was either the
        //      same at every pixel or provably absent from the answer: the
        //      fill sample for a solid colour, the rounded-corner test on a
        //      square rect, and the destination read under an opaque source.
        //      Upstream vercel-labs/native#442.
        //   8. It also ran on one core of four. The fork splits rows into
        //      bands. Measured together with 7, scrolling this feed: present
        //      26.4ms to 7.1ms a frame, 23fps to 55fps.
        //      Upstream vercel-labs/native#443.
        //
        // It is v0.10.1 plus those eight patches and nothing else, so any of
        // them can be dropped on its own when it lands upstream. PATCHES.md in the fork is the tracker: one row per patch
        // with its upstream issue and how to remove it. Nothing watches those
        // issues automatically; the list is short enough to read.
        //
        // Note the revert is not one line. src/tests.zig calls hasColorGlyphs()
        // and colorLayerCount(), which are the fork's own API, so Plaza does
        // not compile against vercel-labs/native until those go too.
        //
        // Also reachable as the tag `plaza-linux-gaps-4`, so the commit this
        // fetches does not depend on one branch surviving. A branch deleted or
        // force-pushed past it takes the dependency down for every cold
        // checkout while a warm Zig cache hides it locally.
        .native_sdk = .{
            .url = "git+https://github.com/zig-nostr/native.git#0eee6e3b09bf6ee054e7c62956097ea033bd062e",
            .hash = "native_sdk-0.1.0-hzDzQj5QsgJf3gwFivco6sig1wjGaQcE_2heLFq5zNAU",
        },
        // The zig-nostr protocol library, linked directly into this app process
        // (it vendors secp256k1 + LMDB). Plaza opens the local store in the
        // render process for zero-copy feed reads, the one-process architecture.
        .nostr = .{
            .url = "https://github.com/zig-nostr/nostr/archive/refs/tags/v0.14.1.tar.gz",
            .hash = "nostr-0.14.1-CMyPzSLDCABUv81rExu84jizBXMixUg9gKteve9P_t0h",
        },
    },
    .paths = .{ "build.zig", "build.zig.zon", "src", "assets", "app.zon" },
}
